LunarData.bitacoras = [{"id":"im-1","nombre":"IM-1","nave":"Odysseus (Nova-C)","operador":"Intuitive Machines","programa":"NASA CLPS","tipo":"fija","sitio":{"nombre":"Malapert A","lat":-80.13,"lon":1.44,"elevacionM":2579,"pendienteDeg":12,"fuentes":["nasa-lro"]},"simulador":{"sitio":"malapert-massif","nota":{"en":"3D relief for reference: Malapert massif, 178 km from the real IM-1 site. That site (80.13° S, about 300 km from the pole) is outside our terrain and Sun–Earth data, which cover 190 km around the pole.","es":"Relieve 3D de referencia: el macizo Malapert, a 178 km del sitio real de IM-1. Ese sitio (80,13° S, a unos 300 km del polo) queda fuera de nuestros datos de relieve, Sol y Tierra, que cubren 190 km alrededor del polo."}},"contactoUtc":"2024-02-22T23:23:00Z","finUtc":"2024-02-29","prevista":{"en":"Approx. 10 days of lunar surface operations","es":"Unos 10 días de operaciones en la superficie"},"real":{"en":"7 days: the NASA instruments stopped seven days after landing","es":"7 días: los instrumentos de la NASA se detuvieron siete días después del alunizaje"},"resumen":{"en":"The first U.S. lander on the Moon since Apollo 17. Its own laser rangefinders could not be used, so it landed guided by a NASA lidar. It came down on a 12° slope, caught a foot and ended up on its side, yet it kept sending data for seven days, until the lunar night took its power.","es":"El primer módulo de Estados Unidos en la Luna desde el Apollo 17. Sus propios telémetros láser no se pudieron usar y bajó guiado por un lidar de la NASA. Se posó en una pendiente de 12°, se le trabó una pata y quedó de lado, pero siguió mandando datos siete días, hasta que la noche lunar le quitó la energía."},"hechos":[{"id":"sitio","utc":null,"precision":"dia","fase":"antes","temas":["sitio","tierra"],"titulo":{"en":"Why this site","es":"Por qué este lugar"},"texto":{"en":"Malapert A was picked as a relatively flat, safe area in the heavily cratered southern highlands, and to learn how to send data to Earth from a place where Earth is low on the horizon.","es":"Se eligió Malapert A por ser una zona relativamente plana y segura entre las tierras altas llenas de cráteres del sur, y para aprender a mandar datos a la Tierra desde un lugar donde la Tierra está baja en el horizonte."},"porQue":{"en":"Near the pole, Earth barely rises above the horizon: a hill or a crater rim can cut the link. That is why the Planner measures Earth visibility at the antenna height.","es":"Cerca del polo la Tierra apenas sube sobre el horizonte: una loma o el borde de un cráter cortan el enlace. Por eso el Planificador mide la Tierra a la altura de la antena."},"fuentes":["nasa-presskit"]},{"id":"laseres","utc":"2024-02-22","precision":"dia","fase":"descenso","temas":["descenso"],"titulo":{"en":"The lasers that were never switched on","es":"Los láseres que nunca se encendieron"},"texto":{"en":"In lunar orbit, hours before landing, the team found that the lander's laser rangefinders would not work: a physical safety switch had not been unlocked before launch. They rerouted the guidance to NASA's Navigation Doppler Lidar, a payload that measures altitude and speed with three laser beams.","es":"En órbita lunar, horas antes del alunizaje, el equipo descubrió que los telémetros láser del módulo no funcionarían: un seguro físico no se había desbloqueado antes del lanzamiento. Desviaron el guiado al lidar Doppler de navegación de la NASA, una carga útil que mide la altura y la velocidad con tres haces láser."},"porQue":{"en":"Without a good altitude measurement the lander does not know when to slow down. Hakuto-R fell in 2023 for exactly that reason.","es":"Sin una buena medida de altura la nave no sabe cuándo frenar. Hakuto-R cayó en 2023 justamente por eso."},"fuentes":["techcrunch-0223"]},{"id":"contacto","utc":"2024-02-22T23:23:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"descenso","temas":["descenso","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"Touchdown near Malapert A","es":"Contacto cerca de Malapert A"},"texto":{"en":"Odysseus touched down at 5:23 p.m. CST. During the descent, the Navigation Doppler Lidar and the Radio Frequency Mass Gauge (a fuel gauge) collected their data.","es":"Odysseus tocó el suelo a las 17:23 (hora central de EE. UU.). Durante el descenso, el lidar Doppler de navegación y el medidor de combustible por radiofrecuencia tomaron sus datos."},"fuentes":["nasa-blog-0222","nasa-ciencia"]},{"id":"de-lado","utc":"2024-02-22T23:23:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"descenso","temas":["terreno","descenso"],"titulo":{"en":"On its side","es":"De lado"},"texto":{"en":"It came down a bit faster than planned and moving sideways. A landing foot is believed to have caught on the uneven ground, and the lander tipped over, coming to rest on its side, propped on a rock.","es":"Bajó un poco más rápido de lo previsto y moviéndose de lado. Se cree que una pata se trabó en el suelo irregular y la nave volcó: quedó de lado, apoyada en una roca."},"porQue":{"en":"A lander on a slope or moving sideways at touchdown can tip. In our simulator, the lander tolerates about 12° under its footpads.","es":"Una nave en pendiente o que toca el suelo moviéndose de lado puede volcar. En nuestro simulador, la nave tolera unos 12° bajo las patas."},"fuentes":["techcrunch-0227","nasa-blog-0228"]},{"id":"instrumentos","utc":"2024-02-23","precision":"dia","hastaUtc":"2024-02-29","fase":"superficie","temas":["ciencia","tierra","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"Instruments at work, lying down","es":"Instrumentos trabajando, tumbados"},"texto":{"en":"The three payloads designed for the surface received data: the ROLSES radio receiver recorded two days of data and picked up shortwave radio from Earth leaking through the ionosphere; Lunar Node 1 tested navigation signals; the SCALPSS cameras took images for several days after landing. Data came down as the lander's orientation allowed.","es":"Las tres cargas pensadas para la superficie recibieron datos: el receptor de radio ROLSES grabó dos días de datos y captó la radio de onda corta de la Tierra que se escapa por la ionosfera; Lunar Node 1 probó señales de navegación; las cámaras SCALPSS tomaron imágenes durante varios días tras el alunizaje. Los datos bajaron según lo permitía la orientación del módulo."},"porQue":{"en":"Lying on its side, the antennas did not point well at Earth: less data could come down. A lander is only useful while it can talk to Earth.","es":"De lado, las antenas no apuntaban bien a la Tierra: bajaban menos datos. Una nave solo sirve mientras puede hablar con la Tierra."},"fuentes":["nasa-ciencia","nasa-blog-0228","rolses-arxiv"]},{"id":"lro","utc":"2024-02-24T18:57:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"superficie","temas":["terreno","sitio"],"titulo":{"en":"Seen from orbit: a 12° slope","es":"Visto desde la órbita: pendiente de 12°"},"texto":{"en":"NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed the lander at 80.13° S, 1.44° E, 2,579 m elevation, inside a degraded crater about one kilometer across. The local terrain is sloped at 12 degrees.","es":"El Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter de la NASA fotografió el módulo en 80,13° S, 1,44° E, a 2579 m de altura, dentro de un cráter degradado de un kilómetro. El terreno ahí tiene 12 grados de pendiente."},"porQue":{"en":"12° is right at the limit our simulator uses for tipping over. It is the same check the simulator now does before the descent.","es":"12° está justo en el límite que usa nuestro simulador para el vuelco. Es la misma comprobación que el simulador hace ahora antes del descenso."},"fuentes":["nasa-lro"]},{"id":"foto-27","utc":"2024-02-27","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["terreno"],"titulo":{"en":"A photo that explains the landing","es":"Una foto que explica el alunizaje"},"texto":{"en":"An image taken with the narrow-field-of-view camera shows a leg that absorbed the first contact, and how the lander gently leaned into the surface, which preserved its ability to send data.","es":"Una imagen de la cámara de campo estrecho muestra una pata que absorbió el primer contacto y cómo la nave se recostó suavemente sobre el suelo, lo que le permitió seguir mandando datos."},"fuentes":["nasa-blog-0228"]},{"id":"datos-28","utc":"2024-02-28","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["datos"],"titulo":{"en":"350 megabits on the ground","es":"350 megabits en la Tierra"},"texto":{"en":"NASA and Intuitive Machines report more than 350 megabits of science data downloaded, ready for analysis.","es":"La NASA e Intuitive Machines informan que se bajaron más de 350 megabits de datos científicos, listos para analizar."},"fuentes":["nasa-blog-0228"]},{"id":"noche","utc":"2024-02-29","precision":"dia","fase":"noche","temas":["sol","energia","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"Goodnight, Odie","es":"Buenas noches, Odie"},"texto":{"en":"Seven days after landing, with the onset of lunar night, the mission ends: the lander was not designed to survive the night. In total, more than 500 megabytes of science, technology and spacecraft data. Its last transmission is an image taken at landing, with the crescent Earth. Before going dark, it is set to call home if it survives the two-week night.","es":"Siete días después del alunizaje, al llegar la noche lunar, la misión termina: el módulo no estaba hecho para sobrevivir a la noche. En total, más de 500 megabytes de datos de ciencia, tecnología y de la nave. Su última transmisión es una imagen tomada al alunizar, con la Tierra en cuarto creciente. Antes de apagarse lo dejan listo para llamar a casa si sobrevive a la noche de dos semanas."},"porQue":{"en":"Without sunlight the solar panels give nothing and the battery runs out. That is the 'longest night against the battery' check of the Planner.","es":"Sin Sol los paneles no dan nada y la batería se agota. Es la comprobación «noche más larga contra la batería» del Planificador."},"fuentes":["nasa-ciencia","universetoday-fin","livescience-odie"]},{"id":"escucha","utc":"2024-03-20","precision":"dia","hastaUtc":"2024-03-23","fase":"noche","temas":["sol","energia"],"titulo":{"en":"Listening for a wake-up call","es":"Esperando que despierte"},"texto":{"en":"When enough sunlight was expected to reach the site again, the team listened for a signal. Three days later they concluded the lander was not charging: Odie would not call home again.","es":"Cuando se esperaba que volviera a llegar bastante Sol al sitio, el equipo escuchó por si llegaba una señal. Tres días después concluyeron que el módulo no se cargaba: Odie no volvería a llamar a casa."},"fuentes":["universetoday-fin"]}],"fuentes":[{"id":"nasa-presskit","titulo":"NASA · CLPS: Intuitive Machines IM-1 Mission (press kit)","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/np-2023-12-016-jsc-clps-im-press-kit-web-508.pdf"},{"id":"nasa-blog-0222","titulo":"NASA Artemis blog · February 2024","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/"},{"id":"nasa-blog-0228","titulo":"NASA · NASA, Intuitive Machines Share Images from the Moon, Provide Science Updates (2024-02-28)","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/"},{"id":"nasa-ciencia","titulo":"NASA · NASA Collects First Surface Science in Decades via Commercial Moon Mission","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/"},{"id":"nasa-lro","titulo":"NASA · NASA's LRO Images Intuitive Machines' Odysseus Lander","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/lro/nasas-lro-images-intuitive-machines-odysseus-lander/"},{"id":"rolses-arxiv","titulo":"Results from NASA's First Radio Telescope on the Moon (ROLSES-1), arXiv:2503.09842","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.09842"},{"id":"techcrunch-0223","titulo":"TechCrunch · Quick thinking and a stroke of luck averted a moon lander disaster for Intuitive Machines (2024-02-23)","url":"https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/23/quick-thinking-and-a-stroke-of-luck-averted-a-moon-lander-disaster-for-intuitive-machines"},{"id":"techcrunch-0227","titulo":"TechCrunch · Intuitive Machines faces early end to moon mission after lander tips over (2024-02-27)","url":"https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/27/intuitive-machines-faces-early-end-to-moon-mission-after-lander-tips-over"},{"id":"universetoday-fin","titulo":"Universe Today · Lunar Night Permanently Ends the Odysseus Mission","url":"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission"},{"id":"livescience-odie","titulo":"Live Science · 'Goodnight, Odie': Odysseus shares farewell image","url":"https://www.livescience.com/space/space-exploration/goodnight-odie-odysseus-the-1st-us-moon-lander-since-apollo-shares-farewell-image-after-lunar-face-plant"}],"markdown":{"en":"# IM-1 · Odysseus (Nova-C)\n\nThe first U.S. lander on the Moon since Apollo 17. Its own laser rangefinders could not be used, so it landed guided by a NASA lidar. It came down on a 12° slope, caught a foot and ended up on its side, yet it kept sending data for seven days, until the lunar night took its power.\n\n- **Operator:** Intuitive Machines\n- **Program:** NASA CLPS\n- **Site:** Malapert A · 80.13° S, 1.44° E · 2579 m · slope 12°\n- **Planned:** Approx. 10 days of lunar surface operations\n- **Actual:** 7 days: the NASA instruments stopped seven days after landing\n\n## Log\n\n### Before the mission — Why this site\n\nMalapert A was picked as a relatively flat, safe area in the heavily cratered southern highlands, and to learn how to send data to Earth from a place where Earth is low on the horizon.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Near the pole, Earth barely rises above the horizon: a hill or a crater rim can cut the link. That is why the Planner measures Earth visibility at the antenna height.\n\nSources: [nasa-presskit](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/np-2023-12-016-jsc-clps-im-press-kit-web-508.pdf)\n\n### Day 1 · 22 Feb 2024 — The lasers that were never switched on\n\nIn lunar orbit, hours before landing, the team found that the lander's laser rangefinders would not work: a physical safety switch had not been unlocked before launch. They rerouted the guidance to NASA's Navigation Doppler Lidar, a payload that measures altitude and speed with three laser beams.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Without a good altitude measurement the lander does not know when to slow down. Hakuto-R fell in 2023 for exactly that reason.\n\nSources: [techcrunch-0223](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/23/quick-thinking-and-a-stroke-of-luck-averted-a-moon-lander-disaster-for-intuitive-machines)\n\n### Day 1 · 22 Feb 2024, 23:23 UTC — Touchdown near Malapert A\n\nOdysseus touched down at 5:23 p.m. CST. During the descent, the Navigation Doppler Lidar and the Radio Frequency Mass Gauge (a fuel gauge) collected their data.\n\nSources: [nasa-blog-0222](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/), [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/)\n\n### Day 1 · 22 Feb 2024, 23:23 UTC — On its side\n\nIt came down a bit faster than planned and moving sideways. A landing foot is believed to have caught on the uneven ground, and the lander tipped over, coming to rest on its side, propped on a rock.\n\n> **Why it matters:** A lander on a slope or moving sideways at touchdown can tip. In our simulator, the lander tolerates about 12° under its footpads.\n\nSources: [techcrunch-0227](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/27/intuitive-machines-faces-early-end-to-moon-mission-after-lander-tips-over), [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Day 2–8 · 23 Feb 2024 → 29 Feb 2024 — Instruments at work, lying down\n\nThe three payloads designed for the surface received data: the ROLSES radio receiver recorded two days of data and picked up shortwave radio from Earth leaking through the ionosphere; Lunar Node 1 tested navigation signals; the SCALPSS cameras took images for several days after landing. Data came down as the lander's orientation allowed.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Lying on its side, the antennas did not point well at Earth: less data could come down. A lander is only useful while it can talk to Earth.\n\nSources: [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/), [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/), [rolses-arxiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.09842)\n\n### Day 3 · 24 Feb 2024, 18:57 UTC — Seen from orbit: a 12° slope\n\nNASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed the lander at 80.13° S, 1.44° E, 2,579 m elevation, inside a degraded crater about one kilometer across. The local terrain is sloped at 12 degrees.\n\n> **Why it matters:** 12° is right at the limit our simulator uses for tipping over. It is the same check the simulator now does before the descent.\n\nSources: [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/lro/nasas-lro-images-intuitive-machines-odysseus-lander/)\n\n### Day 6 · 27 Feb 2024 — A photo that explains the landing\n\nAn image taken with the narrow-field-of-view camera shows a leg that absorbed the first contact, and how the lander gently leaned into the surface, which preserved its ability to send data.\n\nSources: [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Day 7 · 28 Feb 2024 — 350 megabits on the ground\n\nNASA and Intuitive Machines report more than 350 megabits of science data downloaded, ready for analysis.\n\nSources: [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Day 8 · 29 Feb 2024 — Goodnight, Odie\n\nSeven days after landing, with the onset of lunar night, the mission ends: the lander was not designed to survive the night. In total, more than 500 megabytes of science, technology and spacecraft data. Its last transmission is an image taken at landing, with the crescent Earth. Before going dark, it is set to call home if it survives the two-week night.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Without sunlight the solar panels give nothing and the battery runs out. That is the 'longest night against the battery' check of the Planner.\n\nSources: [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/), [universetoday-fin](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission), [livescience-odie](https://www.livescience.com/space/space-exploration/goodnight-odie-odysseus-the-1st-us-moon-lander-since-apollo-shares-farewell-image-after-lunar-face-plant)\n\n### Day 28–31 · 20 Mar 2024 → 23 Mar 2024 — Listening for a wake-up call\n\nWhen enough sunlight was expected to reach the site again, the team listened for a signal. Three days later they concluded the lander was not charging: Odie would not call home again.\n\nSources: [universetoday-fin](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission)\n\n## Sources\n\n- **nasa-presskit** · [NASA · CLPS: Intuitive Machines IM-1 Mission (press kit)](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/np-2023-12-016-jsc-clps-im-press-kit-web-508.pdf)\n- **nasa-blog-0222** · [NASA Artemis blog · February 2024](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/)\n- **nasa-blog-0228** · [NASA · NASA, Intuitive Machines Share Images from the Moon, Provide Science Updates (2024-02-28)](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n- **nasa-ciencia** · [NASA · NASA Collects First Surface Science in Decades via Commercial Moon Mission](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/)\n- **nasa-lro** · [NASA · NASA's LRO Images Intuitive Machines' Odysseus Lander](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/lro/nasas-lro-images-intuitive-machines-odysseus-lander/)\n- **rolses-arxiv** · [Results from NASA's First Radio Telescope on the Moon (ROLSES-1), arXiv:2503.09842](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.09842)\n- **techcrunch-0223** · [TechCrunch · Quick thinking and a stroke of luck averted a moon lander disaster for Intuitive Machines (2024-02-23)](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/23/quick-thinking-and-a-stroke-of-luck-averted-a-moon-lander-disaster-for-intuitive-machines)\n- **techcrunch-0227** · [TechCrunch · Intuitive Machines faces early end to moon mission after lander tips over (2024-02-27)](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/27/intuitive-machines-faces-early-end-to-moon-mission-after-lander-tips-over)\n- **universetoday-fin** · [Universe Today · Lunar Night Permanently Ends the Odysseus Mission](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission)\n- **livescience-odie** · [Live Science · 'Goodnight, Odie': Odysseus shares farewell image](https://www.livescience.com/space/space-exploration/goodnight-odie-odysseus-the-1st-us-moon-lander-since-apollo-shares-farewell-image-after-lunar-face-plant)\n\n*Compiled by Horizon Signai from the sources listed. The «why it matters» notes are our explanation, not mission data.*\n","es":"# IM-1 · Odysseus (Nova-C)\n\nEl primer módulo de Estados Unidos en la Luna desde el Apollo 17. Sus propios telémetros láser no se pudieron usar y bajó guiado por un lidar de la NASA. Se posó en una pendiente de 12°, se le trabó una pata y quedó de lado, pero siguió mandando datos siete días, hasta que la noche lunar le quitó la energía.\n\n- **Operador:** Intuitive Machines\n- **Programa:** NASA CLPS\n- **Sitio:** Malapert A · 80,13° S, 1,44° E · 2579 m · pendiente 12°\n- **Previsto:** Unos 10 días de operaciones en la superficie\n- **Real:** 7 días: los instrumentos de la NASA se detuvieron siete días después del alunizaje\n\n## Bitácora\n\n### Antes de la misión — Por qué este lugar\n\nSe eligió Malapert A por ser una zona relativamente plana y segura entre las tierras altas llenas de cráteres del sur, y para aprender a mandar datos a la Tierra desde un lugar donde la Tierra está baja en el horizonte.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Cerca del polo la Tierra apenas sube sobre el horizonte: una loma o el borde de un cráter cortan el enlace. Por eso el Planificador mide la Tierra a la altura de la antena.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-presskit](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/np-2023-12-016-jsc-clps-im-press-kit-web-508.pdf)\n\n### Día 1 · 22 feb 2024 — Los láseres que nunca se encendieron\n\nEn órbita lunar, horas antes del alunizaje, el equipo descubrió que los telémetros láser del módulo no funcionarían: un seguro físico no se había desbloqueado antes del lanzamiento. Desviaron el guiado al lidar Doppler de navegación de la NASA, una carga útil que mide la altura y la velocidad con tres haces láser.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Sin una buena medida de altura la nave no sabe cuándo frenar. Hakuto-R cayó en 2023 justamente por eso.\n\nFuentes: [techcrunch-0223](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/23/quick-thinking-and-a-stroke-of-luck-averted-a-moon-lander-disaster-for-intuitive-machines)\n\n### Día 1 · 22 feb 2024, 23:23 UTC — Contacto cerca de Malapert A\n\nOdysseus tocó el suelo a las 17:23 (hora central de EE. UU.). Durante el descenso, el lidar Doppler de navegación y el medidor de combustible por radiofrecuencia tomaron sus datos.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-blog-0222](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/), [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/)\n\n### Día 1 · 22 feb 2024, 23:23 UTC — De lado\n\nBajó un poco más rápido de lo previsto y moviéndose de lado. Se cree que una pata se trabó en el suelo irregular y la nave volcó: quedó de lado, apoyada en una roca.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Una nave en pendiente o que toca el suelo moviéndose de lado puede volcar. En nuestro simulador, la nave tolera unos 12° bajo las patas.\n\nFuentes: [techcrunch-0227](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/27/intuitive-machines-faces-early-end-to-moon-mission-after-lander-tips-over), [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Día 2–8 · 23 feb 2024 → 29 feb 2024 — Instrumentos trabajando, tumbados\n\nLas tres cargas pensadas para la superficie recibieron datos: el receptor de radio ROLSES grabó dos días de datos y captó la radio de onda corta de la Tierra que se escapa por la ionosfera; Lunar Node 1 probó señales de navegación; las cámaras SCALPSS tomaron imágenes durante varios días tras el alunizaje. Los datos bajaron según lo permitía la orientación del módulo.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** De lado, las antenas no apuntaban bien a la Tierra: bajaban menos datos. Una nave solo sirve mientras puede hablar con la Tierra.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/), [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/), [rolses-arxiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.09842)\n\n### Día 3 · 24 feb 2024, 18:57 UTC — Visto desde la órbita: pendiente de 12°\n\nEl Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter de la NASA fotografió el módulo en 80,13° S, 1,44° E, a 2579 m de altura, dentro de un cráter degradado de un kilómetro. El terreno ahí tiene 12 grados de pendiente.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** 12° está justo en el límite que usa nuestro simulador para el vuelco. Es la misma comprobación que el simulador hace ahora antes del descenso.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/lro/nasas-lro-images-intuitive-machines-odysseus-lander/)\n\n### Día 6 · 27 feb 2024 — Una foto que explica el alunizaje\n\nUna imagen de la cámara de campo estrecho muestra una pata que absorbió el primer contacto y cómo la nave se recostó suavemente sobre el suelo, lo que le permitió seguir mandando datos.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Día 7 · 28 feb 2024 — 350 megabits en la Tierra\n\nLa NASA e Intuitive Machines informan que se bajaron más de 350 megabits de datos científicos, listos para analizar.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-blog-0228](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n\n### Día 8 · 29 feb 2024 — Buenas noches, Odie\n\nSiete días después del alunizaje, al llegar la noche lunar, la misión termina: el módulo no estaba hecho para sobrevivir a la noche. En total, más de 500 megabytes de datos de ciencia, tecnología y de la nave. Su última transmisión es una imagen tomada al alunizar, con la Tierra en cuarto creciente. Antes de apagarse lo dejan listo para llamar a casa si sobrevive a la noche de dos semanas.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Sin Sol los paneles no dan nada y la batería se agota. Es la comprobación «noche más larga contra la batería» del Planificador.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-ciencia](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/), [universetoday-fin](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission), [livescience-odie](https://www.livescience.com/space/space-exploration/goodnight-odie-odysseus-the-1st-us-moon-lander-since-apollo-shares-farewell-image-after-lunar-face-plant)\n\n### Día 28–31 · 20 mar 2024 → 23 mar 2024 — Esperando que despierte\n\nCuando se esperaba que volviera a llegar bastante Sol al sitio, el equipo escuchó por si llegaba una señal. Tres días después concluyeron que el módulo no se cargaba: Odie no volvería a llamar a casa.\n\nFuentes: [universetoday-fin](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission)\n\n## Fuentes\n\n- **nasa-presskit** · [NASA · CLPS: Intuitive Machines IM-1 Mission (press kit)](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/np-2023-12-016-jsc-clps-im-press-kit-web-508.pdf)\n- **nasa-blog-0222** · [NASA Artemis blog · February 2024](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/)\n- **nasa-blog-0228** · [NASA · NASA, Intuitive Machines Share Images from the Moon, Provide Science Updates (2024-02-28)](https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/artemis/2024/02/28/nasa-intuitive-machines-share-images-from-the-moon-provide-science-updates/)\n- **nasa-ciencia** · [NASA · NASA Collects First Surface Science in Decades via Commercial Moon Mission](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/clps/nasa-collects-first-surface-science-in-decades-via-commercial-moon-mission/)\n- **nasa-lro** · [NASA · NASA's LRO Images Intuitive Machines' Odysseus Lander](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/lro/nasas-lro-images-intuitive-machines-odysseus-lander/)\n- **rolses-arxiv** · [Results from NASA's First Radio Telescope on the Moon (ROLSES-1), arXiv:2503.09842](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.09842)\n- **techcrunch-0223** · [TechCrunch · Quick thinking and a stroke of luck averted a moon lander disaster for Intuitive Machines (2024-02-23)](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/23/quick-thinking-and-a-stroke-of-luck-averted-a-moon-lander-disaster-for-intuitive-machines)\n- **techcrunch-0227** · [TechCrunch · Intuitive Machines faces early end to moon mission after lander tips over (2024-02-27)](https://techcrunch.com/2024/02/27/intuitive-machines-faces-early-end-to-moon-mission-after-lander-tips-over)\n- **universetoday-fin** · [Universe Today · Lunar Night Permanently Ends the Odysseus Mission](https://www.universetoday.com/articles/lunar-night-permanently-ends-the-odysseus-mission)\n- **livescience-odie** · [Live Science · 'Goodnight, Odie': Odysseus shares farewell image](https://www.livescience.com/space/space-exploration/goodnight-odie-odysseus-the-1st-us-moon-lander-since-apollo-shares-farewell-image-after-lunar-face-plant)\n\n*Recopilada por Horizon Signai a partir de las fuentes indicadas. Las notas «por qué importa» son explicación nuestra, no datos de la misión.*\n"}},{"id":"blue-ghost-1","nombre":"Blue Ghost M1","nave":"Blue Ghost","operador":"Firefly Aerospace","programa":"NASA CLPS","tipo":"fija","sitio":{"nombre":"Mare Crisium · Mons Latreille","lat":18.562,"lon":61.81,"fuentes":["nssdc"]},"simulador":{"sitio":"connecting-ridge-roi-6","nota":{"en":"3D relief for reference: Connecting Ridge, at the south pole. The real site is in Mare Crisium (18.56° N, 61.81° E), far from the pole and outside our terrain data.","es":"Relieve 3D de referencia: Connecting Ridge, en el polo sur. El sitio real está en Mare Crisium (18,56° N, 61,81° E), lejos del polo y fuera de nuestros datos de relieve."}},"contactoUtc":"2025-03-02T08:34:00Z","finUtc":"2025-03-16","prevista":{"en":"About one lunar day: some 14 Earth days","es":"Alrededor de un día lunar: unos 14 días terrestres"},"real":{"en":"More than 14 days: 346 hours of daylight and just over 5 hours into the lunar night","es":"Más de 14 días: 346 horas de luz y algo más de 5 horas ya en la noche lunar"},"resumen":{"en":"The one that went right. Blue Ghost landed upright in Mare Crisium with ten NASA instruments and worked a whole lunar day: it drilled, tracked GPS from the Moon, watched Earth eclipse the Sun and filmed the sunset, then kept going five hours into the night.","es":"La que salió bien. Blue Ghost alunizó derecha en Mare Crisium con diez instrumentos de la NASA y trabajó un día lunar entero: perforó, siguió el GPS desde la Luna, vio a la Tierra eclipsar al Sol y filmó el atardecer, y aún siguió cinco horas ya de noche."},"hechos":[{"id":"sitio","utc":null,"precision":"dia","fase":"antes","temas":["sitio","sol"],"titulo":{"en":"Why this site","es":"Por qué este lugar"},"texto":{"en":"Mare Crisium is a basin about 550 kilometers wide, formed by an asteroid impact and later flooded with basaltic lava: flat ground. The instruments were to operate for about one lunar day, some 14 Earth days.","es":"Mare Crisium es una cuenca de unos 550 kilómetros, formada por el impacto de un asteroide y después inundada de lava basáltica: terreno llano. Los instrumentos debían operar alrededor de un día lunar, unos 14 días terrestres."},"porQue":{"en":"Far from the poles the Sun climbs high and stays up 14 days in a row: easy power, and then 14 days of night. At the pole it is the other way round: low Sun, but short nights on the right ridge.","es":"Lejos de los polos el Sol sube alto y dura 14 días seguidos: energía fácil, y luego 14 días de noche. En el polo es al revés: Sol bajo, pero noches cortas en la cresta correcta."},"fuentes":["nasa-contacto"]},{"id":"contacto","utc":"2025-03-02T08:34:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"superficie","temas":["descenso","terreno"],"titulo":{"en":"Touchdown, upright","es":"Contacto, y derecha"},"texto":{"en":"Blue Ghost landed at 3:34 a.m. EST near Mons Latreille, in Mare Crisium, with its ten NASA instruments. The SCALPSS cameras captured the descent and how the engine plume disturbed the surface.","es":"Blue Ghost alunizó a las 3:34 a. m. (hora del este de EE. UU.) cerca de Mons Latreille, en Mare Crisium, con sus diez instrumentos de la NASA. Las cámaras SCALPSS captaron el descenso y cómo el chorro del motor alteró la superficie."},"porQue":{"en":"Flat ground and a low, wide lander: nothing to tip over. Compare it with IM-1 and IM-2, tall landers on polar slopes.","es":"Terreno llano y una nave baja y ancha: nada que la vuelque. Compárala con IM-1 e IM-2, naves altas en pendientes polares."},"fuentes":["nasa-contacto","firefly-fin"]},{"id":"suelo","utc":"2025-03-02","precision":"dia","hastaUtc":"2025-03-16","fase":"superficie","temas":["ciencia"],"titulo":{"en":"Into the ground","es":"Hacia dentro del suelo"},"texto":{"en":"LISTER drilled about three feet into the surface to measure the temperature and the heat flowing out of the Moon. Lunar PlanetVac collected and sorted regolith with bursts of nitrogen gas. The magnetotelluric sounder laid four electrodes up to 60 feet from the lander.","es":"LISTER perforó cerca de un metro (tres pies) para medir la temperatura y el calor que sale del interior de la Luna. Lunar PlanetVac recogió y clasificó regolito con chorros de nitrógeno. El sondeador magnetotelúrico tendió cuatro electrodos hasta a 18 metros (60 pies) de la nave."},"porQue":{"en":"These measurements take days, not minutes: that is why the mission needed a full lunar day of sunlight.","es":"Estas medidas llevan días, no minutos: por eso la misión necesitaba un día lunar entero de Sol."},"fuentes":["firefly-fin"]},{"id":"senales","utc":"2025-03-02","precision":"dia","hastaUtc":"2025-03-16","fase":"superficie","temas":["tierra","ciencia"],"titulo":{"en":"Signals from Earth, on the Moon","es":"Señales de la Tierra, en la Luna"},"texto":{"en":"LuGRE acquired and tracked navigation satellite signals (GPS and Galileo) on the way to the Moon and on its surface, a first. A retroreflector returned laser pulses fired from observatories on Earth, and LEXI took X-ray images of the solar wind meeting Earth's magnetic field.","es":"LuGRE captó y siguió señales de satélites de navegación (GPS y Galileo) camino a la Luna y en su superficie, algo que nunca se había hecho. Un retrorreflector devolvió pulsos láser disparados desde observatorios en la Tierra, y LEXI tomó imágenes en rayos X del viento solar al chocar con el campo magnético terrestre."},"porQue":{"en":"All of this needs Earth in the sky. From Mare Crisium, on the near side, Earth never sets; at the pole it sits on the horizon and the terrain can hide it.","es":"Todo esto necesita la Tierra en el cielo. Desde Mare Crisium, en la cara visible, la Tierra nunca se pone; en el polo queda sobre el horizonte y el relieve puede taparla."},"fuentes":["firefly-fin"]},{"id":"eclipse","utc":"2025-03-14","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["sol","energia"],"titulo":{"en":"Earth eclipses the Sun","es":"La Tierra eclipsa al Sol"},"texto":{"en":"Blue Ghost captured high-definition images of a total solar eclipse seen from the Moon: Earth blocked the Sun and its shadow fell on the lunar surface. Three instruments kept measuring how dust and radiation changed.","es":"Blue Ghost captó imágenes en alta definición de un eclipse total de Sol visto desde la Luna: la Tierra tapó al Sol y su sombra cayó sobre la superficie lunar. Tres instrumentos siguieron midiendo cómo cambiaban el polvo y la radiación."},"porQue":{"en":"During the eclipse the panels give nothing: the lander lived on its battery, as it would in any night.","es":"Durante el eclipse los paneles no dan nada: la nave vivió de su batería, como en cualquier noche."},"fuentes":["firefly-fin"]},{"id":"atardecer","utc":"2025-03-16","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["sol"],"titulo":{"en":"Sunset on the Moon","es":"Atardecer en la Luna"},"texto":{"en":"The lander filmed the lunar sunset, to learn whether dust lifts off the ground and makes the horizon glow.","es":"La nave filmó el atardecer lunar, para saber si el polvo se levanta del suelo y hace brillar el horizonte."},"fuentes":["firefly-fin"]},{"id":"noche","utc":"2025-03-16T23:15:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"noche","temas":["sol","energia","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"Five hours into the night","es":"Cinco horas de noche"},"texto":{"en":"After 346 hours of daylight, Blue Ghost kept working just over 5 hours into the lunar night, on battery. The last data arrived around 6:15 p.m. CDT. In total, more than 119 gigabytes, 51 of them science and technology data.","es":"Tras 346 horas de luz, Blue Ghost siguió trabajando algo más de 5 horas ya en la noche lunar, con batería. Los últimos datos llegaron hacia las 6:15 p. m. (hora central de EE. UU.). En total, más de 119 gigabytes, 51 de ellos de ciencia y tecnología."},"porQue":{"en":"Five hours is what its battery gave. The Planner asks you the same number: how many hours without Sun your mission can take.","es":"Cinco horas es lo que dio su batería. El Planificador te pide ese mismo número: cuántas horas sin Sol aguanta tu misión."},"fuentes":["firefly-fin"]}],"fuentes":[{"id":"nasa-contacto","titulo":"NASA · Touchdown! Carrying NASA Science, Firefly's Blue Ghost Lands on Moon (2025-03-02)","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/"},{"id":"firefly-fin","titulo":"Firefly Aerospace · Firefly Aerospace Successfully Completes 14 Days of Surface Operations on the Moon (2025-03-17)","url":"https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon"},{"id":"nssdc","titulo":"NASA NSSDCA · Blue Ghost Mission 1 (landing coordinates)","url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=BLUEGHOST"}],"markdown":{"en":"# Blue Ghost M1 · Blue Ghost\n\nThe one that went right. Blue Ghost landed upright in Mare Crisium with ten NASA instruments and worked a whole lunar day: it drilled, tracked GPS from the Moon, watched Earth eclipse the Sun and filmed the sunset, then kept going five hours into the night.\n\n- **Operator:** Firefly Aerospace\n- **Program:** NASA CLPS\n- **Site:** Mare Crisium · Mons Latreille · 18.56° N, 61.81° E\n- **Planned:** About one lunar day: some 14 Earth days\n- **Actual:** More than 14 days: 346 hours of daylight and just over 5 hours into the lunar night\n\n## Log\n\n### Before the mission — Why this site\n\nMare Crisium is a basin about 550 kilometers wide, formed by an asteroid impact and later flooded with basaltic lava: flat ground. The instruments were to operate for about one lunar day, some 14 Earth days.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Far from the poles the Sun climbs high and stays up 14 days in a row: easy power, and then 14 days of night. At the pole it is the other way round: low Sun, but short nights on the right ridge.\n\nSources: [nasa-contacto](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/)\n\n### Day 1 · 2 Mar 2025, 08:34 UTC — Touchdown, upright\n\nBlue Ghost landed at 3:34 a.m. EST near Mons Latreille, in Mare Crisium, with its ten NASA instruments. The SCALPSS cameras captured the descent and how the engine plume disturbed the surface.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Flat ground and a low, wide lander: nothing to tip over. Compare it with IM-1 and IM-2, tall landers on polar slopes.\n\nSources: [nasa-contacto](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/), [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Day 1–15 · 2 Mar 2025 → 16 Mar 2025 — Into the ground\n\nLISTER drilled about three feet into the surface to measure the temperature and the heat flowing out of the Moon. Lunar PlanetVac collected and sorted regolith with bursts of nitrogen gas. The magnetotelluric sounder laid four electrodes up to 60 feet from the lander.\n\n> **Why it matters:** These measurements take days, not minutes: that is why the mission needed a full lunar day of sunlight.\n\nSources: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Day 1–15 · 2 Mar 2025 → 16 Mar 2025 — Signals from Earth, on the Moon\n\nLuGRE acquired and tracked navigation satellite signals (GPS and Galileo) on the way to the Moon and on its surface, a first. A retroreflector returned laser pulses fired from observatories on Earth, and LEXI took X-ray images of the solar wind meeting Earth's magnetic field.\n\n> **Why it matters:** All of this needs Earth in the sky. From Mare Crisium, on the near side, Earth never sets; at the pole it sits on the horizon and the terrain can hide it.\n\nSources: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Day 13 · 14 Mar 2025 — Earth eclipses the Sun\n\nBlue Ghost captured high-definition images of a total solar eclipse seen from the Moon: Earth blocked the Sun and its shadow fell on the lunar surface. Three instruments kept measuring how dust and radiation changed.\n\n> **Why it matters:** During the eclipse the panels give nothing: the lander lived on its battery, as it would in any night.\n\nSources: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Day 15 · 16 Mar 2025 — Sunset on the Moon\n\nThe lander filmed the lunar sunset, to learn whether dust lifts off the ground and makes the horizon glow.\n\nSources: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Day 15 · 16 Mar 2025, 23:15 UTC — Five hours into the night\n\nAfter 346 hours of daylight, Blue Ghost kept working just over 5 hours into the lunar night, on battery. The last data arrived around 6:15 p.m. CDT. In total, more than 119 gigabytes, 51 of them science and technology data.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Five hours is what its battery gave. The Planner asks you the same number: how many hours without Sun your mission can take.\n\nSources: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n## Sources\n\n- **nasa-contacto** · [NASA · Touchdown! Carrying NASA Science, Firefly's Blue Ghost Lands on Moon (2025-03-02)](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/)\n- **firefly-fin** · [Firefly Aerospace · Firefly Aerospace Successfully Completes 14 Days of Surface Operations on the Moon (2025-03-17)](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n- **nssdc** · [NASA NSSDCA · Blue Ghost Mission 1 (landing coordinates)](https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=BLUEGHOST)\n\n*Compiled by Horizon Signai from the sources listed. The «why it matters» notes are our explanation, not mission data.*\n","es":"# Blue Ghost M1 · Blue Ghost\n\nLa que salió bien. Blue Ghost alunizó derecha en Mare Crisium con diez instrumentos de la NASA y trabajó un día lunar entero: perforó, siguió el GPS desde la Luna, vio a la Tierra eclipsar al Sol y filmó el atardecer, y aún siguió cinco horas ya de noche.\n\n- **Operador:** Firefly Aerospace\n- **Programa:** NASA CLPS\n- **Sitio:** Mare Crisium · Mons Latreille · 18,56° N, 61,81° E\n- **Previsto:** Alrededor de un día lunar: unos 14 días terrestres\n- **Real:** Más de 14 días: 346 horas de luz y algo más de 5 horas ya en la noche lunar\n\n## Bitácora\n\n### Antes de la misión — Por qué este lugar\n\nMare Crisium es una cuenca de unos 550 kilómetros, formada por el impacto de un asteroide y después inundada de lava basáltica: terreno llano. Los instrumentos debían operar alrededor de un día lunar, unos 14 días terrestres.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Lejos de los polos el Sol sube alto y dura 14 días seguidos: energía fácil, y luego 14 días de noche. En el polo es al revés: Sol bajo, pero noches cortas en la cresta correcta.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-contacto](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/)\n\n### Día 1 · 2 mar 2025, 08:34 UTC — Contacto, y derecha\n\nBlue Ghost alunizó a las 3:34 a. m. (hora del este de EE. UU.) cerca de Mons Latreille, en Mare Crisium, con sus diez instrumentos de la NASA. Las cámaras SCALPSS captaron el descenso y cómo el chorro del motor alteró la superficie.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Terreno llano y una nave baja y ancha: nada que la vuelque. Compárala con IM-1 e IM-2, naves altas en pendientes polares.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-contacto](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/), [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Día 1–15 · 2 mar 2025 → 16 mar 2025 — Hacia dentro del suelo\n\nLISTER perforó cerca de un metro (tres pies) para medir la temperatura y el calor que sale del interior de la Luna. Lunar PlanetVac recogió y clasificó regolito con chorros de nitrógeno. El sondeador magnetotelúrico tendió cuatro electrodos hasta a 18 metros (60 pies) de la nave.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Estas medidas llevan días, no minutos: por eso la misión necesitaba un día lunar entero de Sol.\n\nFuentes: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Día 1–15 · 2 mar 2025 → 16 mar 2025 — Señales de la Tierra, en la Luna\n\nLuGRE captó y siguió señales de satélites de navegación (GPS y Galileo) camino a la Luna y en su superficie, algo que nunca se había hecho. Un retrorreflector devolvió pulsos láser disparados desde observatorios en la Tierra, y LEXI tomó imágenes en rayos X del viento solar al chocar con el campo magnético terrestre.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Todo esto necesita la Tierra en el cielo. Desde Mare Crisium, en la cara visible, la Tierra nunca se pone; en el polo queda sobre el horizonte y el relieve puede taparla.\n\nFuentes: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Día 13 · 14 mar 2025 — La Tierra eclipsa al Sol\n\nBlue Ghost captó imágenes en alta definición de un eclipse total de Sol visto desde la Luna: la Tierra tapó al Sol y su sombra cayó sobre la superficie lunar. Tres instrumentos siguieron midiendo cómo cambiaban el polvo y la radiación.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Durante el eclipse los paneles no dan nada: la nave vivió de su batería, como en cualquier noche.\n\nFuentes: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Día 15 · 16 mar 2025 — Atardecer en la Luna\n\nLa nave filmó el atardecer lunar, para saber si el polvo se levanta del suelo y hace brillar el horizonte.\n\nFuentes: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n### Día 15 · 16 mar 2025, 23:15 UTC — Cinco horas de noche\n\nTras 346 horas de luz, Blue Ghost siguió trabajando algo más de 5 horas ya en la noche lunar, con batería. Los últimos datos llegaron hacia las 6:15 p. m. (hora central de EE. UU.). En total, más de 119 gigabytes, 51 de ellos de ciencia y tecnología.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Cinco horas es lo que dio su batería. El Planificador te pide ese mismo número: cuántas horas sin Sol aguanta tu misión.\n\nFuentes: [firefly-fin](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n\n## Fuentes\n\n- **nasa-contacto** · [NASA · Touchdown! Carrying NASA Science, Firefly's Blue Ghost Lands on Moon (2025-03-02)](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/touchdown-carrying-nasa-science-fireflys-blue-ghost-lands-on-moon/)\n- **firefly-fin** · [Firefly Aerospace · Firefly Aerospace Successfully Completes 14 Days of Surface Operations on the Moon (2025-03-17)](https://fireflyspace.com/news/firefly-aerospace-successfully-completes-14-days-of-surface-operations-on-the-moon)\n- **nssdc** · [NASA NSSDCA · Blue Ghost Mission 1 (landing coordinates)](https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=BLUEGHOST)\n\n*Recopilada por Horizon Signai a partir de las fuentes indicadas. Las notas «por qué importa» son explicación nuestra, no datos de la misión.*\n"}},{"id":"im-2","nombre":"IM-2","nave":"Athena (Nova-C)","operador":"Intuitive Machines","programa":"NASA CLPS","tipo":"fija","sitio":{"nombre":"Mons Mouton","lat":-84.7906,"lon":29.1957,"fuentes":["lroc"]},"simulador":{"sitio":"im-2"},"contactoUtc":"2025-03-06T17:30:00Z","finUtc":"2025-03-07","prevista":{"en":"10 days of surface operations","es":"10 días de operaciones en la superficie"},"real":{"en":"Closer to 10 hours: the batteries ran out before it could recharge","es":"Más cerca de 10 horas: las baterías se agotaron antes de poder recargar"},"resumen":{"en":"Athena landed closer to the Moon's south pole than any lander before it, carrying a drill to look for ice. It came down inside a crater, more than 400 meters from its target, and ended up on its side. With the Sun low, the panels pointing the wrong way and the cold of the crater, it could not recharge: the mission ended the next day.","es":"Athena alunizó más cerca del polo sur de la Luna que ninguna nave antes, con un taladro para buscar hielo. Cayó dentro de un cráter, a más de 400 metros de su objetivo, y quedó de lado. Con el Sol bajo, los paneles mal orientados y el frío del cráter, no pudo recargar: la misión terminó al día siguiente."},"hechos":[{"id":"sitio","utc":null,"precision":"dia","fase":"antes","temas":["sitio","ciencia"],"titulo":{"en":"Why this site","es":"Por qué este lugar"},"texto":{"en":"Mons Mouton is a plateau near the Moon's south pole. Athena carried PRIME-1: a drill (TRIDENT) and a mass spectrometer (MSOLO) to measure what is in the ground below the surface, including water ice.","es":"Mons Mouton es una meseta cerca del polo sur de la Luna. Athena llevaba PRIME-1: un taladro (TRIDENT) y un espectrómetro de masas (MSOLO) para medir qué hay en el suelo bajo la superficie, incluido hielo de agua."},"porQue":{"en":"Ice near the pole would mean water, air and fuel for a future base without bringing them from Earth. That is why so many missions aim here.","es":"Hielo cerca del polo significaría agua, aire y combustible para una base futura sin traerlos de la Tierra. Por eso tantas misiones apuntan aquí."},"fuentes":["nasa-taladro","nasa-fin"]},{"id":"contacto","utc":"2025-03-06","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["descenso","sitio","terreno"],"titulo":{"en":"Touchdown inside a crater","es":"Contacto dentro de un cráter"},"texto":{"en":"Athena touched down at approximately 11:30 a.m. on March 6, inside a crater, more than 1,300 feet (400 meters) from its intended landing site. It is closer to the south pole than any lander before it.","es":"Athena tocó el suelo hacia las 11:30 a. m. del 6 de marzo, dentro de un cráter, a más de 400 metros (1300 pies) de su punto de alunizaje previsto. Es la nave que más cerca del polo sur había alunizado."},"porQue":{"en":"400 meters is enough to change everything: the planned spot was lit; the crater floor was not. A small change of place changes the Sun you get.","es":"400 metros bastan para cambiarlo todo: el punto previsto tenía luz; el fondo del cráter, no. Un pequeño cambio de lugar cambia el Sol que recibes."},"fuentes":["nasa-fin","nasa-lro"]},{"id":"de-lado","utc":"2025-03-06","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["terreno","energia"],"titulo":{"en":"On its side, again","es":"De lado, otra vez"},"texto":{"en":"Images sent later confirmed the lander was on its side, which kept it from fully operating the drill and the other instruments.","es":"Las imágenes que mandó después confirmaron que la nave estaba de lado, lo que le impidió operar del todo el taladro y los demás instrumentos."},"porQue":{"en":"It is the same fate as IM-1 a year earlier: a tall lander on rough polar ground. The terrain under the footpads decides the mission.","es":"Es el mismo destino que IM-1 un año antes: una nave alta sobre terreno polar irregular. El suelo bajo las patas decide la misión."},"fuentes":["nasa-fin"]},{"id":"taladro","utc":"2025-03-06","precision":"dia","fase":"superficie","temas":["ciencia","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"The drill moves, on the Moon","es":"El taladro se mueve, en la Luna"},"texto":{"en":"Even so, TRIDENT and MSOLO started right up. Commanded from Earth, the drill rotated its auger, extended to its full range and hammered. MSOLO made several scans and detected gases, likely from the lander's own propulsion system.","es":"Aun así, TRIDENT y MSOLO arrancaron a la primera. Con órdenes desde la Tierra, el taladro giró su barrena, se extendió del todo y martilló. MSOLO hizo varios barridos y detectó gases, probablemente del propio sistema de propulsión de la nave."},"porQue":{"en":"Every command went up by radio and every reading came down the same way: the hours the instruments worked were hours with Earth in view and battery left.","es":"Cada orden subió por radio y cada lectura bajó igual: las horas que trabajaron los instrumentos fueron horas con la Tierra a la vista y batería disponible."},"fuentes":["nasa-taladro","nasa-fin"]},{"id":"fin","utc":"2025-03-07T06:15:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"noche","temas":["sol","energia","datos"],"titulo":{"en":"It cannot recharge","es":"No puede recargar"},"texto":{"en":"At 12:15 a.m. CST Intuitive Machines called an early end of mission, with the batteries depleted. With the direction of the Sun, the orientation of the solar panels and the extreme cold in the crater, the company did not expect Athena to recharge. The plan was 10 days; it got closer to 10 hours, and about 6.6 gigabytes of data.","es":"A las 12:15 a. m. (hora central de EE. UU.) Intuitive Machines dio por terminada la misión antes de tiempo, con las baterías agotadas. Por la dirección del Sol, la orientación de los paneles solares y el frío extremo del cráter, la empresa no esperaba que Athena recargara. El plan era de 10 días; fueron más cerca de 10 horas, y unos 6,6 gigabytes de datos."},"porQue":{"en":"Direction of the Sun and where the panel looks: that is exactly what the Planner computes for a site, a date and a panel height.","es":"Dirección del Sol y hacia dónde mira el panel: es justo lo que el Planificador calcula para un lugar, una fecha y una altura de panel."},"fuentes":["nasa-fin","nasa-taladro","sciam"]},{"id":"lro","utc":"2025-03-07T17:54:00Z","precision":"minuto","fase":"noche","temas":["sitio"],"titulo":{"en":"Seen from orbit","es":"Visto desde la órbita"},"texto":{"en":"NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter imaged the site at 12:54 p.m. EST on March 7, about a day after landing: the lander is near the center of Mons Mouton.","es":"El Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter de la NASA fotografió el sitio a las 12:54 p. m. (hora del este de EE. UU.) del 7 de marzo, cerca de un día después del alunizaje: la nave está cerca del centro de Mons Mouton."},"fuentes":["nasa-lro"]}],"fuentes":[{"id":"nasa-fin","titulo":"NASA · NASA Receives Some Data Before Intuitive Machines Ends Lunar Mission (2025-03-07)","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/"},{"id":"nasa-taladro","titulo":"NASA · NASA's Lunar Drill Technology Passes Tests on the Moon","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/"},{"id":"nasa-lro","titulo":"NASA · NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Views IM-2 on Moon's Surface","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/"},{"id":"lroc","titulo":"LROC (Arizona State University) · IM-2 landing site (coordinates as in our site catalog)","url":"https://www.lroc.asu.edu/images/1401"},{"id":"sciam","titulo":"Scientific American · Athena Lunar Lander Declared Dead on the Moon","url":"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/athena-lunar-lander-declared-dead-on-the-moon/"}],"markdown":{"en":"# IM-2 · Athena (Nova-C)\n\nAthena landed closer to the Moon's south pole than any lander before it, carrying a drill to look for ice. It came down inside a crater, more than 400 meters from its target, and ended up on its side. With the Sun low, the panels pointing the wrong way and the cold of the crater, it could not recharge: the mission ended the next day.\n\n- **Operator:** Intuitive Machines\n- **Program:** NASA CLPS\n- **Site:** Mons Mouton · 84.79° S, 29.20° E\n- **Planned:** 10 days of surface operations\n- **Actual:** Closer to 10 hours: the batteries ran out before it could recharge\n\n## Log\n\n### Before the mission — Why this site\n\nMons Mouton is a plateau near the Moon's south pole. Athena carried PRIME-1: a drill (TRIDENT) and a mass spectrometer (MSOLO) to measure what is in the ground below the surface, including water ice.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Ice near the pole would mean water, air and fuel for a future base without bringing them from Earth. That is why so many missions aim here.\n\nSources: [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Day 1 · 6 Mar 2025 — Touchdown inside a crater\n\nAthena touched down at approximately 11:30 a.m. on March 6, inside a crater, more than 1,300 feet (400 meters) from its intended landing site. It is closer to the south pole than any lander before it.\n\n> **Why it matters:** 400 meters is enough to change everything: the planned spot was lit; the crater floor was not. A small change of place changes the Sun you get.\n\nSources: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/), [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n\n### Day 1 · 6 Mar 2025 — On its side, again\n\nImages sent later confirmed the lander was on its side, which kept it from fully operating the drill and the other instruments.\n\n> **Why it matters:** It is the same fate as IM-1 a year earlier: a tall lander on rough polar ground. The terrain under the footpads decides the mission.\n\nSources: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Day 1 · 6 Mar 2025 — The drill moves, on the Moon\n\nEven so, TRIDENT and MSOLO started right up. Commanded from Earth, the drill rotated its auger, extended to its full range and hammered. MSOLO made several scans and detected gases, likely from the lander's own propulsion system.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Every command went up by radio and every reading came down the same way: the hours the instruments worked were hours with Earth in view and battery left.\n\nSources: [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Day 2 · 7 Mar 2025, 06:15 UTC — It cannot recharge\n\nAt 12:15 a.m. CST Intuitive Machines called an early end of mission, with the batteries depleted. With the direction of the Sun, the orientation of the solar panels and the extreme cold in the crater, the company did not expect Athena to recharge. The plan was 10 days; it got closer to 10 hours, and about 6.6 gigabytes of data.\n\n> **Why it matters:** Direction of the Sun and where the panel looks: that is exactly what the Planner computes for a site, a date and a panel height.\n\nSources: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/), [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [sciam](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/athena-lunar-lander-declared-dead-on-the-moon/)\n\n### Day 2 · 7 Mar 2025, 17:54 UTC — Seen from orbit\n\nNASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter imaged the site at 12:54 p.m. EST on March 7, about a day after landing: the lander is near the center of Mons Mouton.\n\nSources: [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n\n## Sources\n\n- **nasa-fin** · [NASA · NASA Receives Some Data Before Intuitive Machines Ends Lunar Mission (2025-03-07)](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n- **nasa-taladro** · [NASA · NASA's Lunar Drill Technology Passes Tests on the Moon](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/)\n- **nasa-lro** · [NASA · NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Views IM-2 on Moon's Surface](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n- **lroc** · [LROC (Arizona State University) · IM-2 landing site (coordinates as in our site catalog)](https://www.lroc.asu.edu/images/1401)\n- **sciam** · [Scientific American · Athena Lunar Lander Declared Dead on the Moon](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/athena-lunar-lander-declared-dead-on-the-moon/)\n\n*Compiled by Horizon Signai from the sources listed. The «why it matters» notes are our explanation, not mission data.*\n","es":"# IM-2 · Athena (Nova-C)\n\nAthena alunizó más cerca del polo sur de la Luna que ninguna nave antes, con un taladro para buscar hielo. Cayó dentro de un cráter, a más de 400 metros de su objetivo, y quedó de lado. Con el Sol bajo, los paneles mal orientados y el frío del cráter, no pudo recargar: la misión terminó al día siguiente.\n\n- **Operador:** Intuitive Machines\n- **Programa:** NASA CLPS\n- **Sitio:** Mons Mouton · 84,79° S, 29,20° E\n- **Previsto:** 10 días de operaciones en la superficie\n- **Real:** Más cerca de 10 horas: las baterías se agotaron antes de poder recargar\n\n## Bitácora\n\n### Antes de la misión — Por qué este lugar\n\nMons Mouton es una meseta cerca del polo sur de la Luna. Athena llevaba PRIME-1: un taladro (TRIDENT) y un espectrómetro de masas (MSOLO) para medir qué hay en el suelo bajo la superficie, incluido hielo de agua.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Hielo cerca del polo significaría agua, aire y combustible para una base futura sin traerlos de la Tierra. Por eso tantas misiones apuntan aquí.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Día 1 · 6 mar 2025 — Contacto dentro de un cráter\n\nAthena tocó el suelo hacia las 11:30 a. m. del 6 de marzo, dentro de un cráter, a más de 400 metros (1300 pies) de su punto de alunizaje previsto. Es la nave que más cerca del polo sur había alunizado.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** 400 metros bastan para cambiarlo todo: el punto previsto tenía luz; el fondo del cráter, no. Un pequeño cambio de lugar cambia el Sol que recibes.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/), [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n\n### Día 1 · 6 mar 2025 — De lado, otra vez\n\nLas imágenes que mandó después confirmaron que la nave estaba de lado, lo que le impidió operar del todo el taladro y los demás instrumentos.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Es el mismo destino que IM-1 un año antes: una nave alta sobre terreno polar irregular. El suelo bajo las patas decide la misión.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Día 1 · 6 mar 2025 — El taladro se mueve, en la Luna\n\nAun así, TRIDENT y MSOLO arrancaron a la primera. Con órdenes desde la Tierra, el taladro giró su barrena, se extendió del todo y martilló. MSOLO hizo varios barridos y detectó gases, probablemente del propio sistema de propulsión de la nave.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Cada orden subió por radio y cada lectura bajó igual: las horas que trabajaron los instrumentos fueron horas con la Tierra a la vista y batería disponible.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n\n### Día 2 · 7 mar 2025, 06:15 UTC — No puede recargar\n\nA las 12:15 a. m. (hora central de EE. UU.) Intuitive Machines dio por terminada la misión antes de tiempo, con las baterías agotadas. Por la dirección del Sol, la orientación de los paneles solares y el frío extremo del cráter, la empresa no esperaba que Athena recargara. El plan era de 10 días; fueron más cerca de 10 horas, y unos 6,6 gigabytes de datos.\n\n> **Por qué importa:** Dirección del Sol y hacia dónde mira el panel: es justo lo que el Planificador calcula para un lugar, una fecha y una altura de panel.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-fin](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/), [nasa-taladro](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/), [sciam](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/athena-lunar-lander-declared-dead-on-the-moon/)\n\n### Día 2 · 7 mar 2025, 17:54 UTC — Visto desde la órbita\n\nEl Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter de la NASA fotografió el sitio a las 12:54 p. m. (hora del este de EE. UU.) del 7 de marzo, cerca de un día después del alunizaje: la nave está cerca del centro de Mons Mouton.\n\nFuentes: [nasa-lro](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n\n## Fuentes\n\n- **nasa-fin** · [NASA · NASA Receives Some Data Before Intuitive Machines Ends Lunar Mission (2025-03-07)](https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/)\n- **nasa-taladro** · [NASA · NASA's Lunar Drill Technology Passes Tests on the Moon](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/nasas-lunar-drill-technology-passes-tests-on-the-moon/)\n- **nasa-lro** · [NASA · NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Views IM-2 on Moon's Surface](https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasas-lunar-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-im-2-on-moons-surface/)\n- **lroc** · [LROC (Arizona State University) · IM-2 landing site (coordinates as in our site catalog)](https://www.lroc.asu.edu/images/1401)\n- **sciam** · [Scientific American · Athena Lunar Lander Declared Dead on the Moon](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/athena-lunar-lander-declared-dead-on-the-moon/)\n\n*Recopilada por Horizon Signai a partir de las fuentes indicadas. Las notas «por qué importa» son explicación nuestra, no datos de la misión.*\n"}}];
